    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Replace a true HEPA filter every 6 to 12 months, replace an activated carbon filter every 3 to 6 months, and vacuum or rinse a washable pre-filter about once a month. Those are the ranges most manufacturers give for a purifier running daily in an ordinary home. Pets, smokers, wildfire smoke, heavy cooking, renovation dust, mold and round-the-clock running all push you toward the short end, and sometimes past it. The filter change light on most purifiers is a timer counting running hours, not a sensor checking the filter, so treat it as a reminder and check the filter yourself. Here is the schedule for each filter type, the signs that one is done, and how to make them last.
        </p>

        <div id="schedule" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Often Should Each Type of Filter Be Changed?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most purifiers have two or three stages, and each wears out on its own schedule. The pre-filter is a coarse mesh that catches hair, lint and large dust. The main filter is usually true HEPA, which catches fine particles. Many units also have an activated carbon layer for odors and gases, either as a separate filter or bonded to the HEPA cartridge.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Filter</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Interval</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Washable pre-filter</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clean every 2 to 4 weeks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Vacuum or rinse; dry fully; replace if torn</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">True HEPA</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Replace every 6 to 12 months</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not washable; shorter with smoke, pets or mold</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Activated carbon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Replace every 3 to 6 months</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Saturates; odor returning is the sign</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Combined HEPA and carbon cartridge</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Replace every 6 to 12 months</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Carbon usually tires before the HEPA does</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Washable permanent filter</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clean as the maker directs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often not true HEPA; replace when damaged</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The HEPA filter carries the most weight. It is the stage that removes the fine particles that matter for allergies, smoke and mold spores, and it cannot be cleaned. As it loads with dust it actually catches particles just as well or better, but it restricts airflow more and more, so the purifier delivers less clean air at every fan speed. Replacing it on schedule is what keeps the unit performing near its rated CADR.
          </p>

          <p>
            Carbon is different. It does not clog the way HEPA does; it fills up. Its surface adsorbs odor and gas molecules until there is no room left, and then it simply stops working, often while still looking clean. In a home with a steady odor source, a thin carbon layer can be spent in weeks; a thick bed of granular carbon lasts much longer.
          </p>

          <p>
            The pre-filter is the cheap insurance. Keeping it clean stops lint and pet hair from blanketing the HEPA layer, which is the single easiest way to get the full life out of the expensive filter.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="shorter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Makes Air Purifier Filters Wear Out Faster?
          </h2>

          <p>
            How much dirt passes through the filter, which comes down to how long the purifier runs and how dirty the air is. A unit running 24 hours a day on medium loads its filter several times faster than one running a few hours on low. Manufacturers usually base their estimate on regular daily use, and some assume a set number of hours a day, so check the manual if yours runs continuously.
          </p>

          <p>
            Smoke is the fastest way to use up a filter. Tobacco smoke, wood smoke and especially wildfire smoke are made of fine particles in enormous numbers, and a few days of heavy wildfire smoke can load a HEPA filter as much as months of ordinary use. Smoke also saturates carbon quickly. Our picks for the <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-smoke/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best air purifiers for smoke</a> favor units with large filters for this reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pets, cooking and construction come next. Dogs and cats shed hair that blankets the pre-filter and dander that loads the HEPA. Frying and searing put out oily particles that coat filters. Sanding, drywall work and demolition can clog a filter in days, so if you run a purifier during a renovation, expect to replace the filter at the end of it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mold and humidity shorten filter life in a different way. A filter that has been catching spores in a damp room is not just loaded; it can become a place for mold to grow. In a basement or a home dealing with mold, we would lean toward the 6 month end for HEPA and inspect it every month or two.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Lifespan Ranges</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1 mo</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Pre-filter cleaning</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">3-6 mo</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Carbon filter</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">6-12 mo</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">True HEPA filter</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Days</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Heavy smoke can load a filter</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="indicator" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Is the Filter Change Light Accurate?
          </h2>

          <p>
            It is a reminder, not a measurement. On most purifiers the filter indicator is a timer that counts how many hours the fan has run and lights up after a set number. Some smarter units weight the count by fan speed, so hours on high use up the timer faster. Very few actually measure airflow or pressure across the filter.
          </p>

          <p>
            That means the light knows nothing about your conditions. A purifier running in a smoky apartment and one running in a clean, pet-free bedroom will light up at the same time if they run the same hours, even though one filter is clogged and the other has plenty left. The light can be early in a clean home and very late in a dirty one.
          </p>

          <p>
            It also only works if you reset it. Most indicators need a button held for a few seconds after a new filter goes in. Forget that and the light either stays on, so you start ignoring it, or it resets at the wrong time and throws off the next cycle. The manual or the label inside the filter door usually explains the reset.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use the light as your prompt to look. When it comes on, or every couple of months in between, pull the filters and check them against the signs below. Then decide whether to replace, rather than letting the timer decide for you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="signs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Can You Tell a Filter Needs Changing?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Look at the whole surface, not just the color. A HEPA filter turns gray from the first weeks of use, and gray alone does not mean it is done. A filter that is dark across the entire intake face, with dust packed into the pleats so you cannot see the media between them, is near the end. So is one that is visibly damaged, torn, crushed or wet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Listen and feel. As the HEPA filter clogs, airflow from the outlet drops noticeably, and some units get louder or develop a whistle as the fan works harder against the resistance. If auto mode seems to run at a high speed far more often than it used to, a loaded filter may be the reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            Smell is the sign for carbon. When cooking smells, pet odors or a musty note start lingering in a room where the purifier used to clear them, the carbon is saturated. A musty or sour smell coming from the purifier itself, especially at startup, is a stronger warning: the filter may be growing mold and should be replaced right away. Our guide on <a href="/can-mold-grow-in-an-air-purifier/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">whether mold can grow in an air purifier</a> covers that situation.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE TWO-MINUTE CHECK</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Look, listen, smell.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Pull the pre-filter and main filter in good light, check that the pleats are not packed solid, feel the airflow at the outlet on medium, and sniff the outlet at startup. If any of the three is off, replace the filter regardless of the indicator.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Your own symptoms count too. If allergy symptoms creep back in a room where the purifier used to help, check the filter before assuming the purifier has stopped working.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mold" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Often Should You Change Filters in a Home With Mold?
          </h2>

          <p>
            More often, and more carefully. A purifier running against mold collects spores continuously, and those spores stay alive in the filter. In a dry room that is harmless, but in a damp basement or a home recovering from a leak, a loaded filter can start growing mold itself. We would replace HEPA filters at the 6 month mark in that situation and carbon filters at 3 months, and inspect both every month or two.
          </p>

          <p>
            Handle old filters as if they are full of spores, because they are. Turn the purifier off, slide the old filter directly into a plastic bag in the same room, seal it and take it outside to the trash. Wear a well-fitting mask if you are sensitive. Never tap, vacuum or rinse a filter that has been running against mold.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pair filter changes with humidity control. Keeping relative humidity between 30 and 50 percent, the range the EPA recommends, protects the filter as well as the house. A purifier in a basement that sits at 70 percent will go through filters fast and may still smell musty. Our guide on <a href="/does-a-hepa-filter-remove-mold-spores/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">whether a HEPA filter removes mold spores</a> explains what the filter can and cannot do for a mold problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose a purifier with filters you can afford to change often. For a mold-prone space, a unit with sealed true HEPA, a real carbon bed and reasonably priced replacements is worth more than one with fancy features and expensive cartridges. Our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-mold/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best air purifiers for mold</a> are picked with running costs in mind.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="washable" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Can You Wash a HEPA Filter Instead of Replacing It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not a true HEPA filter. True HEPA media is a mat of extremely fine fibers, often glass fiber, arranged so it catches at least 99.97 percent of particles at 0.3 microns. Water, brushing and vacuuming can damage those fibers, open gaps and flush trapped particles deeper into the mat. Most manufacturers say plainly not to wash them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Filters sold as washable or permanent HEPA are usually a different product. Many are labeled HEPA-type or HEPA-style, which has no fixed performance requirement, and their efficiency may be well below true HEPA from the start. Some are reasonably good; the point is that you cannot assume they match the true HEPA standard, and repeated washing can lower whatever efficiency they have.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you do have a washable filter, follow the maker's instructions exactly, and dry it completely before it goes back in, which can mean a full day or more. A damp filter in a running purifier is one of the most reliable ways to grow mold in one. That matters most in exactly the homes where people buy purifiers for mold.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our view: for allergies, smoke or mold, use replaceable true HEPA filters and accept the cost. A permanent filter can make sense for general dust in a dry room, but it is not a way to get HEPA performance for free. Our guide on <a href="/do-air-purifiers-help-with-mold/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">whether air purifiers help with mold</a> explains why filtration quality matters in a damp home.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="save" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Can You Make Filters Last Longer and Cost Less?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clean the pre-filter faithfully. Vacuuming or rinsing it every two to four weeks, and letting it dry fully, keeps hair and lint off the HEPA layer. In a home with shedding pets it is the difference between a HEPA filter lasting six months and lasting twelve. It costs nothing but a minute.
          </p>

          <p>
            Run the purifier smartly. Auto mode or a low setting moves less air through the filter when the room is already clean, so the filter loads more slowly without giving up much air quality. Close windows on smoky and high-pollen days so the purifier is not filtering the outdoors. A better HVAC filter, MERV 11 to 13 if your system can handle it, takes some load off the portable unit too.
          </p>

          <p>
            Shop carefully for replacements. Genuine filters from the manufacturer fit and seal properly, which matters because air leaking around a filter is not cleaned. Third-party filters are often cheaper and some are fine, but check that they claim true HEPA rather than HEPA-type, and that they fit snugly. Buying filters in multi-packs usually lowers the per-filter price.
          </p>

          <p>
            And factor filters into the purchase. A year of filters often costs more than a year of electricity, which our breakdown of <a href="/how-much-electricity-does-an-air-purifier-use/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how much electricity an air purifier uses</a> shows. Before buying a purifier, look up the replacement price and multiply by how many you will need each year. That number, not the sticker price, tells you what the unit really costs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Waiting for the indicator light</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most filter lights are timers that count running hours. They do not know if you have pets, a smoker or a week of wildfire smoke. Inspect the filters every couple of months and whenever the room starts smelling or feeling stuffy, and replace based on what you see, hear and smell.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Washing a true HEPA filter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water, brushing and vacuuming damage the fine fibers in true HEPA media and push trapped particles deeper in. Unless the manufacturer specifically says the filter is washable, replace it. Clean only the pre-filter, which is designed for it, and let it dry completely before refitting.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting to reset the timer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                After fitting a new filter, most purifiers need the indicator reset, usually by holding a button for a few seconds. Skip it and the light stays on until you stop noticing it, which means you miss the next change entirely. Check the manual or the label inside the filter door.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the carbon filter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Carbon stops absorbing odors once it is saturated, often while still looking clean. If smells linger where the purifier used to clear them, the carbon is done, even if the HEPA filter has months left. Replace carbon every 3 to 6 months, sooner with smoke, pets or a musty basement.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a HEPA-type replacement to save money</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cheap third-party filters labeled HEPA-type or HEPA-style may catch far fewer fine particles and may not seal properly in the housing. Saving a few dollars is not worth losing the filtration you bought the purifier for. Look for true HEPA and a snug, gasketed fit.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if you never change an air purifier filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Airflow drops steadily as the HEPA filter clogs, so the purifier delivers less and less clean air even though it sounds like it is working. Carbon stops absorbing odors entirely once saturated. In a damp room, an old filter can start growing mold and blow musty air. Eventually the purifier is mostly just a fan using electricity.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a HEPA filter work better as it gets dirty?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Its particle capture efficiency stays high and can even rise slightly as the layer of trapped dust helps catch more. The problem is airflow. A loaded filter lets much less air through, so the purifier cleans fewer cubic feet per minute and the room gets less clean air overall. That drop in delivered air is why you replace it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do air purifier filters last if I run it 24/7?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually toward the shorter end of the manufacturer's range, so roughly 6 to 9 months for a HEPA filter and 3 to 4 months for carbon in an ordinary home. Smoke, pets, cooking and humidity shorten that further. Check the manual, since some makers base their filter life on a set number of hours per day.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I vacuum a HEPA filter to extend its life?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most manufacturers advise against it. A vacuum can damage the delicate fiber mat and pull particles out unevenly, and it does not restore the filter the way people hope. Vacuum the pre-filter instead, which is designed for it. If your manual specifically allows light vacuuming of the main filter, follow its exact instructions.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are third-party replacement filters safe to use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many are fine, but quality varies. Look for a listing that claims true HEPA, meaning 99.97 percent at 0.3 microns, rather than HEPA-type, and check that the filter seals properly in the housing, because air leaking around the edge is not filtered. Using a non-genuine filter may also affect some warranties, so check yours first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to change the filter if I only use the purifier occasionally?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, eventually. A filter used occasionally loads more slowly, but carbon can still absorb odors from the room while idle, and a filter sitting in a humid space can develop mold. Inspect it at least every 6 months, and replace HEPA filters at least once every year or two even with light use.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I dispose of an old air purifier filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In most areas, used purifier filters go in household trash. Slide the filter into a plastic bag before taking it out of the room, especially if it has been catching mold, smoke or pet dander, and seal the bag. Check with your local waste service if you are unsure, and do not shake out or burn old filters.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

